WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.135 --> 00:00:03.402
-Corporate profits
and CEO pay are soaring

00:00:03.402 --> 00:00:04.737
despite the president's promise

00:00:04.737 --> 00:00:06.406
to be a champion
of the working class.

00:00:06.406 --> 00:00:08.574
For more on this, it's time
for "A Closer Look."

00:00:08.574 --> 00:00:10.042
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:10.042 --> 00:00:12.678 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:12.678 --> 00:00:15.447
Donald Trump has many
weird rhetorical tics.

00:00:15.447 --> 00:00:16.916
He knows like five words.

00:00:16.916 --> 00:00:19.519
He'll never correct himself if
he mispronounces something.

00:00:19.519 --> 00:00:21.588
And in general,
he sounds like Joe Pesci

00:00:21.588 --> 00:00:24.223
after five shots of novocaine.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:24.223 --> 00:00:26.492
But one of the weirdest
rhetorical tics he has

00:00:26.492 --> 00:00:29.195
is a story he likes to repeat
at public events.

00:00:29.195 --> 00:00:32.031
He keeps saying that
big, strong, tough guys

00:00:32.031 --> 00:00:34.634
are coming up to him and crying
because he's saving the economy.

00:00:34.634 --> 00:00:36.001
He's done it many, many times.

00:00:36.001 --> 00:00:37.569
And he repeated it again
during a roundtable

00:00:37.569 --> 00:00:38.904
on the economy this week.

00:00:38.904 --> 00:00:42.741
-A strong man came up to me --
a tough kind of a guy,

00:00:42.741 --> 00:00:46.812
and said, "I want to thank you,
Mr. President,

00:00:46.812 --> 00:00:48.514
for saving our country."

00:00:48.514 --> 00:00:50.016
-Yes!
-Whoo!

00:00:50.016 --> 00:00:53.185
-And he had tears
coming down his eyes.

00:00:53.185 --> 00:00:55.354
Men that were tough and strong,

00:00:55.354 --> 00:00:56.922
women that were tough
and strong,

00:00:56.922 --> 00:00:59.258
they see me, there are tears
coming down their eyes.

00:00:59.258 --> 00:01:01.094
A man came up to me,
and he was crying --

00:01:01.094 --> 00:01:03.763
A strong man, big, tough guy,
but he was crying.

00:01:03.763 --> 00:01:07.967
I've had such an incredible
experience with the miners.

00:01:07.967 --> 00:01:12.004
Of the nine,
eight of them were crying.

00:01:12.004 --> 00:01:17.009
Steel workers came up to me,
and they were crying.

00:01:17.009 --> 00:01:18.544
Big people from big companies,

00:01:18.544 --> 00:01:20.079
they've been to the White House
50 times.

00:01:20.079 --> 00:01:21.548
In one case, I won't say who,

00:01:21.548 --> 00:01:23.783
the person came into
the oval office

00:01:23.783 --> 00:01:25.118
and started to cry.

00:01:25.118 --> 00:01:27.720
-I mean, there are so many
things that are weird

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:29.522
about Donald Trump,
but this is one of the weirdest.

00:01:29.522 --> 00:01:33.092
Who are these big, strong,
tough guys

00:01:33.092 --> 00:01:35.194
who are supposedly
coming up to him and crying?

00:01:35.194 --> 00:01:36.262
[ As Trump ]
You got to see them,

00:01:36.262 --> 00:01:38.397
they're big, muscular
weightlifters,

00:01:38.397 --> 00:01:39.999
and, I mean,
they're just bawling.

00:01:39.999 --> 00:01:42.835
One of them came up to me,
tears streaming down his face,

00:01:42.835 --> 00:01:44.904
and he asked me a question.
It broke my heart.

00:01:44.904 --> 00:01:46.406
He said, "Can you...

00:01:46.406 --> 00:01:48.941
smell what The Rock is cooking?
And I --

00:01:48.941 --> 00:01:54.547
[ Laughter, cheers,
and applause ]

00:01:54.547 --> 00:01:56.048
[ Normal voice ] Trump,
of course, tells these stories

00:01:56.048 --> 00:01:58.318
because he's spent his political
career cultivating the myth

00:01:58.318 --> 00:02:00.820
that he is a hero
to the working class,

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:03.522
while in reality, as we found
out yet again recently,

00:02:03.522 --> 00:02:06.025
he and the political elite
he claims to hate

00:02:06.025 --> 00:02:08.160
have overseen an economy
which corporate profits

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:09.562
and CEO pay are soaring.

00:02:09.562 --> 00:02:11.130
In fact, one of the most
profitable companies

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:13.233
in the world is even getting
a tax refund.

00:02:13.233 --> 00:02:15.267
-And NBC News report reveals

00:02:15.267 --> 00:02:17.603
that twice as many
corporations --

00:02:17.603 --> 00:02:21.206
Are you ready for this? --
are paying zero taxes

00:02:21.206 --> 00:02:23.042
under President Trump.

00:02:23.042 --> 00:02:25.177
-A new analysis found
twice as many companies

00:02:25.177 --> 00:02:28.348
paid nothing in taxes last year
under the Trump tax law.

00:02:28.348 --> 00:02:30.717
The number jumped from 30 to 60.

00:02:30.717 --> 00:02:34.720
-This includes major companies
like Amazon, Netflix, and Delta.

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:38.191
-In 2018, Amazon reported
more than $11 billion

00:02:38.191 --> 00:02:42.829
in taxable income, and paid
zero dollars in federal taxes.

00:02:42.829 --> 00:02:44.063
And it's even worse than that --

00:02:44.063 --> 00:02:46.665
Amazon is actually getting
a federal tax refund

00:02:46.665 --> 00:02:49.202
of $120 million this year.

00:02:49.202 --> 00:02:51.271
-I'm sorry, I don't care
if you're a capitalist

00:02:51.271 --> 00:02:52.772
or a socialist or whatever,

00:02:52.772 --> 00:02:54.540
but if one of the richest
companies in the world

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:56.275
run by one of the richest men
in the world

00:02:56.275 --> 00:02:59.111
is getting a tax refund,
then the system is [bleep] up.

00:02:59.111 --> 00:03:04.850
I mean, Amazon --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:08.454
Amazon shouldn't get a refund.

00:03:08.454 --> 00:03:12.859
It's where regular people go
when they get their refund.

00:03:12.859 --> 00:03:14.960
"Well, thank you, Uncle Sam,

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:18.164
looks like someone's getting
that new Brita filter."

00:03:18.164 --> 00:03:19.332
[ Laughter ]

00:03:19.332 --> 00:03:21.333
The imbalance between
what corporate CEOs make

00:03:21.333 --> 00:03:23.502
and what their employees make
is staggering and insane.

00:03:23.502 --> 00:03:24.537
And to demonstrate that,

00:03:24.537 --> 00:03:26.205
California Congresswoman
Katie Porter

00:03:26.205 --> 00:03:28.040
a former professor
of consumer law

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:30.275
decided to do a simple
math problem during a hearing

00:03:30.275 --> 00:03:33.145
last week with
JPMorgan Chase CEO Jamie Dimon.

00:03:33.145 --> 00:03:35.748
This year, Dimon got a raise
to $31 million.

00:03:35.748 --> 00:03:38.050
So Porter gave Dimon
a budget scenario

00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:40.286
involving a bank teller who
lives in Porter's district

00:03:40.286 --> 00:03:42.255
to make an entry level salary.

00:03:42.255 --> 00:03:44.857
Porter asked Dimon how he would
deal with that budget,

00:03:44.857 --> 00:03:46.292
and he did not have an answer.

00:03:46.292 --> 00:03:48.294
Here's just the first part
of that exchange.

00:03:48.294 --> 00:03:51.764
She had $2,425 a month.

00:03:51.764 --> 00:03:53.566
She rents a one-bedroom
apartment.

00:03:53.566 --> 00:03:56.736
That average one-bedroom
apartment is going to be $1,600.

00:03:56.736 --> 00:04:01.807
She spends $100 on utilities,
$400 for car expenses and gas.

00:04:01.807 --> 00:04:03.977
A low food budget is $400.

00:04:03.977 --> 00:04:05.345
She has a Cricket cellphone,

00:04:05.345 --> 00:04:07.680
the cheapest cellphone
she can get for $40.

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:09.782
She has afterschool childcare
because the bank is open

00:04:09.782 --> 00:04:11.250
during normal business hours.

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:12.552
That's $450 a month.

00:04:12.552 --> 00:04:17.723
That takes her down to
negative $567 per month.

00:04:17.723 --> 00:04:20.692
My question for you, Mr. Dimon,
is how should she manage

00:04:20.692 --> 00:04:22.395
this budget shortfall

00:04:22.395 --> 00:04:25.131
while she's working full time
at your bank?

00:04:25.131 --> 00:04:26.732
-I, uh...

00:04:26.732 --> 00:04:28.834
I don't know that all
the these numbers are accurate.

00:04:28.834 --> 00:04:31.971
That number is generally
a starter job.

00:04:31.971 --> 00:04:34.139
She is a starting employee.
She has a 6-year-old child.

00:04:34.139 --> 00:04:36.142
This is her first job.
-You can get those jobs

00:04:36.142 --> 00:04:38.944
out of high school,
and she may have my job one day.

00:04:38.944 --> 00:04:40.813
-Maybe the teller
would be better

00:04:40.813 --> 00:04:42.081
at running your bank
than you are.

00:04:42.081 --> 00:04:44.883
Under Dimon, JPMorgan has been
fined $2.8 million

00:04:44.883 --> 00:04:46.719
over improper safeguards
for customers,

00:04:46.719 --> 00:04:49.122
$65 million for trying
to rig a benchmark rate,

00:04:49.122 --> 00:04:52.959
$135 million from the SEC,
$13 billion over bad mortgages,

00:04:52.959 --> 00:04:56.095
and another $1 billion for just
a bunch of other scandals.

00:04:56.095 --> 00:04:58.797
And on top of that, every time
I go to your bank,

00:04:58.797 --> 00:05:00.633
the pens have been stolen
from the chains.

00:05:00.633 --> 00:05:02.568
And how am I supposed
to fill out

00:05:02.568 --> 00:05:03.736
a deposit slip without a pen?

00:05:03.736 --> 00:05:05.171
Am I supposed to bring my own
pen to the bank?

00:05:05.171 --> 00:05:06.405
Because no one carries pens
anymore.

00:05:06.405 --> 00:05:08.774
Either have more pens
or get stronger chains.

00:05:08.774 --> 00:05:10.877
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:05:10.877 --> 00:05:15.180
Also, during that exchange,

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:17.950
Dimon actually told Porter
those numbers aren't accurate.

00:05:17.950 --> 00:05:20.086
So Porter responded by posting
a photo of herself

00:05:20.086 --> 00:05:21.387
on Twitter with a white board

00:05:21.387 --> 00:05:22.922
proving that her numbers
were accurate.

00:05:22.922 --> 00:05:25.557
Look at that.
She literally showed her work.

00:05:25.557 --> 00:05:28.794
This is the first time
anyone in Congress

00:05:28.794 --> 00:05:30.529
has ever gotten extra credit.

00:05:30.529 --> 00:05:34.033
Usually the only math
members of Congress are doing

00:05:34.033 --> 00:05:35.501
is counting sheep.

00:05:35.501 --> 00:05:39.538
-It's about trying to do what's
right for the whole country.

00:05:39.538 --> 00:05:43.842
And if your heart doesn't break
when you...

00:05:43.842 --> 00:05:47.513
-[ Laughs ] Look at him.

00:05:47.513 --> 00:05:51.117
He just realized, "Oh, [bleep]

00:05:51.117 --> 00:05:54.587
I fell asleep live on C-SPAN."

00:05:54.587 --> 00:05:56.388
And based on that camera work,

00:05:56.388 --> 00:05:59.058
C-SPAN realized that was
the most exciting thing

00:05:59.058 --> 00:06:01.060
that ever happened on C-SPAN.

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:02.862
"Someone's asleep,
take camera five!"

00:06:02.862 --> 00:06:07.599
"We only have one camera."
"Move camera one!"

00:06:07.599 --> 00:06:08.868
But Porter kept going.

00:06:08.868 --> 00:06:11.337
She pressed Dimon on how
he would solve her constituents'

00:06:11.337 --> 00:06:13.506
budget shortfall,
and he still would not answer.

00:06:13.506 --> 00:06:15.207
-She doesn't have the ability
right now

00:06:15.207 --> 00:06:17.209
to spend your $31 million.

00:06:17.209 --> 00:06:20.813
She's short $567. What would you
suggest she should do?

00:06:20.813 --> 00:06:23.149
-I don't know.
I have to think about that.

00:06:23.149 --> 00:06:26.752
-Would you recommend she take
out a JPMorgan Chase credit card

00:06:26.752 --> 00:06:28.654
and run a deficit?

00:06:28.654 --> 00:06:30.656
-I don't know.
I'd have to think about it.

00:06:30.656 --> 00:06:33.192
-Would you recommend
that she overdraft at your bank

00:06:33.192 --> 00:06:36.062
and be charged overdraft fees?

00:06:36.062 --> 00:06:38.164
-I don't know.
I'd have to think about it.

00:06:38.164 --> 00:06:40.599
-So, I know you have a lot of --
-I'd love to call her up

00:06:40.599 --> 00:06:42.801
and have a conversation about
her financial affairs

00:06:42.801 --> 00:06:44.837
and see if we can be helpful.

00:06:44.837 --> 00:06:46.438
-See if you can find a way
for her to live

00:06:46.438 --> 00:06:49.141
on less than the minimum
that I've described?

00:06:49.141 --> 00:06:50.643
-Just be helpful.

00:06:50.643 --> 00:06:53.112
-You're acting like it's some
unsolvable math problem

00:06:53.112 --> 00:06:54.647
instead of just paying her
more money.

00:06:54.647 --> 00:06:56.381
Dimon's going to be up all night
at a chalkboard

00:06:56.381 --> 00:06:58.751
trying to crack this one
"Good Will Hunting" style.

00:06:58.751 --> 00:07:02.755
[ As Dimon ] Let's see, she has
$2,000, and I have $31 million.

00:07:02.755 --> 00:07:05.157
Damn it, numbers,
why won't you crunch?!

00:07:05.157 --> 00:07:06.592
[ Laughter ]

00:07:06.592 --> 00:07:09.361
[ Normal voice ] Now,
the reason these companies

00:07:09.361 --> 00:07:12.632
get away with this is basically
just because we let them.

00:07:12.632 --> 00:07:15.000
That's it. They have teams
of lawyers that help them

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:16.368
use deductions and loopholes,

00:07:16.368 --> 00:07:18.437
and many of them stash
their profits offshore,

00:07:18.437 --> 00:07:20.106
and that's why
Senator Elizabeth Warren

00:07:20.106 --> 00:07:21.440
unveiled a new plan recently

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:24.043
to specifically target companies
that don't pay taxes.

00:07:24.043 --> 00:07:26.278
-Today, Elizabeth Warren,
the senator from Massachusetts,

00:07:26.278 --> 00:07:28.146
rolled out a plan to tax
big corporations

00:07:28.146 --> 00:07:29.715
that use loopholes
in the tax code.

00:07:29.715 --> 00:07:32.318
She tweeted, "Giant companies
like Amazon shouldn't be able

00:07:32.318 --> 00:07:34.052
to get away with paying
zero dollars

00:07:34.052 --> 00:07:35.554
in federal corporate income
taxes.

00:07:35.554 --> 00:07:38.791
-Her plan is called
the Real Corporate Profits Tax,

00:07:38.791 --> 00:07:41.127
and it focuses on raising
the tax rate for companies

00:07:41.127 --> 00:07:43.829
that make over $100 million.

00:07:43.829 --> 00:07:46.298
-We've got a corporate tax rate,
but as you know,

00:07:46.298 --> 00:07:48.500
the thing has just been
lobbied to death.

00:07:48.500 --> 00:07:52.104
It's full of loopholes
and breaks and special deals

00:07:52.104 --> 00:07:56.108
so that a company like Amazon
that makes $10 billion

00:07:56.108 --> 00:07:59.177
in profits can walk away
paying nothing in taxes.

00:07:59.177 --> 00:08:00.813
That's just not fair.

00:08:00.813 --> 00:08:02.681
-I love the idea that
Warren is taking on

00:08:02.681 --> 00:08:05.083
one of the most valuable
public companies in the world.

00:08:05.083 --> 00:08:07.720
She should publish her plan
on Amazon

00:08:07.720 --> 00:08:10.021
just so we can see the
recommendations below it.

00:08:10.021 --> 00:08:12.257
"You might also like,
'Jailing Wall Street Bankers,'

00:08:12.257 --> 00:08:14.426
or 'Bernie Sanders
yelling at a billionaire.'"

00:08:14.426 --> 00:08:15.961
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:08:15.961 --> 00:08:18.830
We're living in a gilded age,

00:08:18.830 --> 00:08:21.067
a massive inequality
that predates Trump.

00:08:21.067 --> 00:08:22.434
It's baked into the system.

00:08:22.434 --> 00:08:24.069
He just lied about it
and made it worse.

00:08:24.069 --> 00:08:26.405
For example, for years,
the media has pointed

00:08:26.405 --> 00:08:28.774
to the stock market
as a sign of economic success.

00:08:28.774 --> 00:08:31.276
And Trump, of course, loves to
brag every chance he gets

00:08:31.276 --> 00:08:33.979
about how well the stock market
has done under his presidency,

00:08:33.979 --> 00:08:35.514
and he did it again this week.

00:08:35.514 --> 00:08:38.250
-We've created a tremendous
stock market.

00:08:38.250 --> 00:08:39.785
People are making
a lot of money.

00:08:39.785 --> 00:08:41.887
Our markets are hitting records
all the time.

00:08:41.887 --> 00:08:45.157
I have many, many records
of the highest stock market.

00:08:45.157 --> 00:08:47.893
The stock market is way up again
today.

00:08:47.893 --> 00:08:51.030
And we're setting a record
literally all the time.

00:08:51.030 --> 00:08:54.633
The stock market is right now
almost at an all-time high.

00:08:54.633 --> 00:08:56.835
I have about 18 records.

00:08:56.835 --> 00:09:00.172
It then went down a little bit
because we had to do

00:09:00.172 --> 00:09:02.407
a couple of trade deals
and people didn't understand.

00:09:02.407 --> 00:09:04.576
Now they're starting to say,
"You know, that was pretty good.

00:09:04.576 --> 00:09:06.279
That's good what he's doing."

00:09:06.279 --> 00:09:08.314
-Oh, they were saying that?

00:09:08.314 --> 00:09:09.381
[ Laughter ]

00:09:09.381 --> 00:09:11.684
Were they also crying?

00:09:11.684 --> 00:09:13.619
[ As Trump ] They were saying,
"It's good what he's doing,

00:09:13.619 --> 00:09:17.756
and they were -- I mean,
tears streaming down their faces

00:09:17.756 --> 00:09:20.125
and then down onto their
jacked torsos.

00:09:20.125 --> 00:09:23.562
These guys were so strong
and so sad.

00:09:23.562 --> 00:09:26.831
Just pumping iron
and crying their eyes out."

00:09:26.831 --> 00:09:28.433
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:09:28.433 --> 00:09:32.471
[ Normal voice ] But the stock
market is not the real economy.

00:09:32.471 --> 00:09:33.772
In fact, it's mostly just a sign

00:09:33.772 --> 00:09:35.274
of how the wealthy few
are faring.

00:09:35.274 --> 00:09:37.509
One study found that nearly
40% of the market

00:09:37.509 --> 00:09:39.878
is owned by the richest
1% of the population,

00:09:39.878 --> 00:09:43.482
while the bottom 90%
of the population owns just 19%

00:09:43.482 --> 00:09:44.983
of stock market wealth.

00:09:44.983 --> 00:09:47.752
This is why it just makes basic
sense to people that the rich

00:09:47.752 --> 00:09:49.155
should pay more in taxes.

00:09:49.155 --> 00:09:51.157
On top of the corporate tax,
Warren also proposed

00:09:51.157 --> 00:09:53.659
a wealth tax on assets
over $50 million.

00:09:53.659 --> 00:09:56.061
And Bernie Sanders even
went on Fox News this week

00:09:56.061 --> 00:09:58.030
to attack Trump's tax bill
during a town hall,

00:09:58.030 --> 00:09:59.865
and he actually got cheers
from the crowd.

00:09:59.865 --> 00:10:04.703
-I happen to believe that
a tax bill written and pushed

00:10:04.703 --> 00:10:09.074
by Trump who told the American
people that that tax bill,

00:10:09.074 --> 00:10:11.610
some of you may recall,
would not benefit the wealthy.

00:10:11.610 --> 00:10:13.378
You remember that?
-Yeah.

00:10:13.378 --> 00:10:15.280
-"Oh, it's not going to benefit
the wealthy."

00:10:15.280 --> 00:10:19.351
83% of the benefits
went to the top 1%.

00:10:19.351 --> 00:10:21.420
So I think that's a bad idea.

00:10:21.420 --> 00:10:24.956
And in my view, people,
whether it's me, you --

00:10:24.956 --> 00:10:27.159
Probably make a lot more money
than I do.

00:10:27.159 --> 00:10:29.695
But whether it's me or you
or anybody else,

00:10:29.695 --> 00:10:32.831
I think wealthy people
and large corporations

00:10:32.831 --> 00:10:34.366
that are making billions
of profits

00:10:34.366 --> 00:10:36.668
should start paying
their fair share of taxes.

00:10:36.668 --> 00:10:39.905
-But Senator --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:10:39.905 --> 00:10:42.775
-And that was on Fox News!

00:10:42.775 --> 00:10:45.110
I mean, talking like that
and getting cheered on Fox News

00:10:45.110 --> 00:10:47.746
is like getting cheered for
praising the Yankees in Boston.

00:10:47.746 --> 00:10:48.947
[ Laughter ]

00:10:48.947 --> 00:10:50.516
[ Boston accent ] He's right.
Jeter's a legend,

00:10:50.516 --> 00:10:53.652
and Starbucks is
a superior option to Dunkies!

00:10:53.652 --> 00:10:55.087
[ Laughter ]

00:10:55.087 --> 00:10:56.722
[ Normal voice ] After that
moment, the Fox News anchors

00:10:56.722 --> 00:10:59.891
asked Bernie why he doesn't
choose to voluntarily pay more

00:10:59.891 --> 00:11:01.227
in taxes than he owes.

00:11:01.227 --> 00:11:02.994
And he turned the question
around on them.

00:11:02.994 --> 00:11:05.564
-Your marginal tax rate was 26%

00:11:05.564 --> 00:11:07.933
because of President Trump's
tax cut.

00:11:07.933 --> 00:11:10.469
So why not say,
I'm leading this revolution,

00:11:10.469 --> 00:11:12.004
I'm not going to take those?

00:11:12.004 --> 00:11:13.439
-[ Scoffs ]

00:11:13.439 --> 00:11:14.973
[ Laughter ]

00:11:14.973 --> 00:11:17.877
Come on. We're nearing --

00:11:17.877 --> 00:11:21.213
I am -- I pay the taxes
that I owe.

00:11:21.213 --> 00:11:23.915
And by the way, why don't you
get Donald Trump up here

00:11:23.915 --> 00:11:26.218
and ask him how much
he pays in taxes.

00:11:26.218 --> 00:11:27.486
[ Cheers and applause ]
-I will.

00:11:27.486 --> 00:11:31.356
Yeah, we'll -- Look.

00:11:31.356 --> 00:11:34.226
-I am eagerly awaiting
your doing that.

00:11:34.226 --> 00:11:39.131
-First of all, there's nothing
better than old man sarcasm.

00:11:39.131 --> 00:11:40.633
[ As Sanders ] Why don't you
put your phone away

00:11:40.633 --> 00:11:42.668
at the dinner table?
-[ Normal voice ] I will.

00:11:42.668 --> 00:11:46.005
-[ As Sanders ] Well, I am
eagerly awaiting you doing that,

00:11:46.005 --> 00:11:48.607
and when you are done,
maybe we can participate

00:11:48.607 --> 00:11:52.511
in the social media I grew up
with called eye contact.

00:11:52.511 --> 00:11:54.346
[ Laughter ]

00:11:54.346 --> 00:11:58.316
[ Normal voice ]
And then after that,

00:11:58.316 --> 00:12:00.752
Bernie directly addressed Trump

00:12:00.752 --> 00:12:03.355
and challenged him
to release his tax returns.

00:12:03.355 --> 00:12:04.890
-I guess the president watches

00:12:04.890 --> 00:12:06.525
your network a little bit,
right?

00:12:06.525 --> 00:12:07.359
[ Laughter ]

00:12:07.359 --> 00:12:08.794
Hey, President Trump.

00:12:08.794 --> 00:12:11.330
My wife and I just released
10 years.

00:12:11.330 --> 00:12:13.399
Please do the same.
Let the American people know.

00:12:13.399 --> 00:12:14.833
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:12:14.833 --> 00:12:17.536
-And you know Trump was watching
that, because, you know,

00:12:17.536 --> 00:12:19.471
he always watches, and also,

00:12:19.471 --> 00:12:22.841
Bernie on Fox News felt like
one of those crossover episodes,

00:12:22.841 --> 00:12:25.677
where a character from one show
goes on another one,

00:12:25.677 --> 00:12:27.746
like when Fonzie went on
"Laverne &amp; Shirley."

00:12:27.746 --> 00:12:30.282
But this is one that
didn't make any sense.

00:12:30.282 --> 00:12:31.884
It was like an episode
of "The Sopranos"

00:12:31.884 --> 00:12:34.653
where Carrie Bradshaw
stopped by the Bada Bing!

00:12:34.653 --> 00:12:36.722
[ Laughter ]

00:12:36.722 --> 00:12:38.690
"I didn't find love
in New Jersey,

00:12:38.690 --> 00:12:41.160
but you can't blame a gada-girl
for trying.

00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:42.628
[ Laughter ]

00:12:42.628 --> 00:12:46.332
One thing's for sure, it's a
night I'll never fuhgeddabout."

00:12:46.332 --> 00:12:49.535
[ "Sex and the City"-esque music
plays ]

00:12:49.535 --> 00:12:51.403
[ Applause ]

00:12:51.403 --> 00:12:52.938
I'm not Carrie.

00:12:52.938 --> 00:12:55.340
[ Laughter ]

00:12:55.340 --> 00:12:57.409
I'm actually Miranda.

00:12:57.409 --> 00:12:58.777
[ Laughter ]

00:12:58.777 --> 00:13:00.779
-We're living in an age
of soaring corporate profits

00:13:00.779 --> 00:13:03.182
and CEO pay, and when
struggling Americans find out

00:13:03.182 --> 00:13:05.884
how much CEOs are making and how
little in taxes they're paying,

00:13:05.884 --> 00:13:07.152
they're all saying the same
thing --

00:13:07.152 --> 00:13:08.353
-[ Scoffs ]

00:13:08.353 --> 00:13:09.755
-This has been "A Closer Look."

00:13:09.755 --> 00:13:11.557
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:13:11.557 --> 00:13:13.559 line:0%
♪♪

